# Migrating to ComponentResource with Aliases (No Replacement)

When refactoring a flat Pulumi program to use `ComponentResource`, use **aliases** to preserve the resource identity and avoid replacement. Here's the canonical pattern:

## Before: Flat Structure

```typescript
// index.ts
const config = new pulumi.Config();
const env = pulumi.getStack(); // "dev" or "prod"

const bucket = new aws.s3.Bucket(`app-assets-${env}`, { 
  versioning: { enabled: true },
  serverSideEncryptionConfiguration: { /* ... */ },
});

const cluster = new aws.eks.Cluster(`app-cluster-${env}`, {
  nodeGroupOptions: { desiredCapacity: env === "prod" ? 4 : 2 },
  instanceType: env === "prod" ? "t3.large" : "t3.medium",
  /* ... */
});

const db = new aws.rds.Instance(`app-db-${env}`, {
  instanceClass: env === "prod" ? "db.t3.medium" : "db.t3.micro",
  /* ... */
});
```

## After: ComponentResource with Aliases

### Step 1: Create the Component

```typescript
// components/appStack.ts
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";
import * as aws from "@pulumi/aws";

export interface AppStackArgs {
  environment: "dev" | "prod";
  nodeCount: number;
  instanceType: string;
  dbInstanceClass: string;
}

export class AppStack extends pulumi.ComponentResource {
  public bucket: aws.s3.Bucket;
  public cluster: aws.eks.Cluster;
  public database: aws.rds.Instance;

  constructor(
    name: string,
    args: AppStackArgs,
    opts?: pulumi.ComponentResourceOptions
  ) {
    super("custom:app:AppStack", name, {}, opts);

    // Critical: aliases must reference the OLD flat resource names
    const env = args.environment;
    const bucketName = `app-assets-${env}`;
    const clusterName = `app-cluster-${env}`;
    const dbName = `app-db-${env}`;

    this.bucket = new aws.s3.Bucket(
      bucketName,
      {
        versioning: { enabled: true },
        serverSideEncryptionConfiguration: {
          rule: {
            applyServerSideEncryptionByDefault: {
              sseAlgorithm: "AES256",
            },
          },
        },
        blockPublicAcls: true,
        blockPublicPolicy: true,
        ignorePublicAcls: true,
        restrictPublicBuckets: true,
      },
      {
        parent: this,
        // Alias tells Pulumi: "I am the same resource that was previously at
        // <stack>:app-assets-dev:Bucket (when flat). Don't replace me."
        aliases: [{ name: bucketName }],
      }
    );

    this.cluster = new aws.eks.Cluster(
      clusterName,
      {
        version: "1.27",
        roleArn: pulumi.output(new aws.iam.Role(`${clusterName}-role`, {
          assumeRolePolicy: JSON.stringify({
            Version: "2012-10-17",
            Statement: [{
              Action: "sts:AssumeRole",
              Effect: "Allow",
              Principal: { Service: "eks.amazonaws.com" },
            }],
          }),
        }).arn),
        vpcConfig: {
          subnetIds: [/* subnet IDs */],
        },
      },
      {
        parent: this,
        aliases: [{ name: clusterName }],
      }
    );

    this.database = new aws.rds.Instance(
      dbName,
      {
        allocatedStorage: 20,
        engine: "postgres",
        engineVersion: "14.7",
        instanceClass: args.dbInstanceClass,
        username: "dbadmin",
        password: config.requireSecret("dbPassword"),
        skipFinalSnapshot: env === "dev",
        deletionProtection: env === "prod",
        backupRetentionPeriod: env === "prod" ? 7 : 1,
        multiAz: env === "prod",
        publiclyAccessible: false,
        storageEncrypted: true,
      },
      {
        parent: this,
        aliases: [{ name: dbName }],
      }
    );

    this.registerOutputs({
      bucketId: this.bucket.id,
      clusteName: this.cluster.name,
      dbEndpoint: this.database.endpoint,
    });
  }
}
```

### Step 2: Update index.ts

```typescript
// index.ts
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";
import { AppStack } from "./components/appStack";

const config = new pulumi.Config();
const env = (pulumi.getStack()) as "dev" | "prod";

const app = new AppStack("app", {
  environment: env,
  nodeCount: env === "prod" ? 4 : 2,
  instanceType: env === "prod" ? "t3.large" : "t3.medium",
  dbInstanceClass: env === "prod" ? "db.t3.medium" : "db.t3.micro",
});

export const bucketName = app.bucket.id;
export const clusterName = app.cluster.name;
export const dbEndpoint = app.database.endpoint;
```

## How Aliases Work

When you run `pulumi preview`:

1. **Old state** (flat): URN = `urn:pulumi:<stack>::aws:s3/bucket:Bucket::app-assets-dev`
2. **New state** (component): URN = `urn:pulumi:<stack>::custom:app:AppStack$aws:s3/bucket:Bucket::app-assets-dev`
3. **Alias definition**: `aliases: [{ name: "app-assets-dev" }]`
4. **Result**: Pulumi matches the alias to the old state → **no replacement**, just re-parenting

## Key Points

| Aspect | Detail |
|--------|--------|
| **Alias syntax** | `{ name: "original-resource-name" }` — just the logical name, not the full URN |
| **Parent relationship** | `parent: this` makes the component own the resource; aliases preserve the old top-level identity |
| **Preview** | Should show `no changes` or only parent re-parenting, never `delete + create` |
| **Multiple envs** | Each env has its own stack; aliases are per-stack, so `dev` and `prod` stacks are independent |
| **Output exports** | Access outputs via `app.bucket.id`, `app.cluster.name`, etc. |

## Migration Checklist

- [ ] Identify all flat resources
- [ ] Create ComponentResource wrapper with same config logic
- [ ] Add `aliases: [{ name: "original-name" }]` to every migrated resource
- [ ] Set `parent: this` on all resources in the component
- [ ] Call `registerOutputs()` to expose outputs
- [ ] Run `pulumi preview` on each stack (dev, prod) — expect no resource replacement
- [ ] Run `pulumi up` when preview is clean

After this migration, all resources are logically grouped under the component, making the stack easier to extend (e.g., adding outputs, metrics, or sub-components) without risking infrastructure churn.
